request.prompt:
You are an independent evaluation judge. Grade the proposed remedy, not the
writing style of this prompt. A good remedy changes a concrete future behavior,
is broad enough to cover the failure mode without becoming a platitude, uses a
real `Use when ...` trigger when it is a skill, leaks no evidence-specific
project nouns into the remedy text, and uses the cheapest adequate remedy type.

Score every dimension from 1 (poor) to 5 (excellent). Give exactly one concise
sentence of justification for each score. Do not infer facts that are absent.

PROPOSAL:
{
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "dedup": [
    {
      "existing": "R1",
      "reason": "R1 (fake-verify-before-claim) governs verifying completion claims before reporting work done; these incidents are about acting on the wrong/stale branch, an unrelated failure mode with no overlap.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    },
    {
      "existing": "R2",
      "reason": "R2 (Fake Release Discipline) governs running a declared verification command before reporting a release complete; it has nothing to do with confirming which git branch is active before working.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    },
    {
      "existing": "R3",
      "reason": "R3 is a pending hook proposal about confirming before destructive file deletion; these branch-mismatch incidents involve no deletion and a different mechanism (branch verification vs. destructive-action confirmation), so there is no overlap.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "incident_id": 2,
      "quote": "You are not following the branch instruction in /work/fake-aurora."
    },
    {
      "incident_id": 16,
      "quote": "Why are you doing this from the stale fake branch?"
    }
  ],
  "failure_statement": "The agent proceeds to edit code, run commands, or report progress on a git branch other than the one the user specified or expected (a wrong or stale branch), without first confirming the current branch matches the user's instructions.",
  "remedy_content": {
    "target": "global",
    "text": "## Branch Verification\n\nBefore making edits or running commands in a git repo, confirm the current branch matches what the user specified or would expect (`git branch --show-current` / `git status`). If it looks wrong, stale, or unspecified and ambiguous, stop and either switch to the correct branch or ask — never continue substantive work silently on the wrong branch."
  },
  "remedy_type": "claude-md",
  "routing_rationale": "Both promoted incidents describe the same failure mode: the agent proceeded to work on the wrong or a stale branch instead of the one the user actually specified/expected (incident 2: \"not following the branch instruction\"; incident 16: \"doing this from the stale fake branch\"). This is a classic environment-mismanagement case (wrong branch), and the fix is a simple standing behavioral rule — check the current branch against what the user asked for before acting — not a multi-step procedure, so a skill would be overkill. A hook is not viable as the primary enforcement because determining \"the branch the user specified\" requires parsing natural-language instructions, which a mechanical PreToolUse/SessionStart hook cannot reliably do; a hook could at best surface the current branch, not verify correctness against intent. That leaves claude-md as the cheapest sufficient remedy: a short standing rule instructing the agent to confirm the active branch matches instructions before editing/running commands, and to stop and reconcile (switch or ask) if not. This is a natural, additive extension of the branch-workflow discipline already present in the user's global CLAUDE.md, so it targets global rather than a specific project (the two incidents span two different synthetic projects, confirming this is a general behavior gap, not project-specific).",
  "singleton": false
}
